<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isère (river)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Isère is a river in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 286 kilometres long, which rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> near the Italian border and flows through the Tarentaise valley and past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenoble">Grenoble</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valence">Valence</a>. In antiquity its valleys were home to Alpine and Gallic peoples such as the Ceutrones and the Allobroges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isere-river.jpg" alt="Isère (river)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 柳沢しげき at Japanese Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grenoble_Isere.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Isère rises in a glacier known as the Sources de l'Isère, in the Vanoise National Park in the Graian Alps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savoie">Savoie</a>, near Val-d'Isère on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Its course measures 286 kilometres. From the Alps it crosses the Pays de Savoie and the Tarentaise valley, cuts between the Chartreuse and Belledonne ranges, follows the Vercors Massif, passes through the Dauphiné and meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vivarais">Vivarais</a>, a few kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valence">Valence</a>. Its main tributaries include the Arc, 127.5 kilometres long, the Drac, 130.3 kilometres, and the Romanche, 78.4 kilometres. Its drainage basin covers 11,890 square kilometres.</p><p>The upper valley is called the Tarentaise and the middle valley the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gresivaudan">Grésivaudan</a>. The lower valley forms part of the Plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valence">Valence</a> and is narrow, no more than 2 kilometres wide. Towns on its banks include Bourg-Saint-Maurice, Moûtiers, Albertville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenoble">Grenoble</a> and Romans-sur-Isère. Many communes have taken the river's name, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Isère is named after it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name was first recorded as Isara, meaning the impetuous one, the swift one. It was not originally a Celtic word but was very likely taken over by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> in ancient times. It is related to the Indo-European root *isərós, meaning impetuous, quick, vigorous, which is similar to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> isiráḥ. The same word figures in many other river names of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and neighbouring lands, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isar">Isar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the Franco-Belgian Yser, the ancient name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oise">Oise</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisack">Isarco</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the Jizera in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>.</p><p>The lower valley's shape comes from the Quaternary glaciations. Alluvium laid down during glaciations, and deepening of the channel in the warmer periods between them, produced stepped terraces on a bed of Miocene molasse, such as the one on which Saint-Marcel-lès-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valence">Valence</a> stands. The terraces still define the geography of the Plain of Valence. The river is fed largely by snowmelt, with spring floods and low water in autumn and winter. Its highest recorded instantaneous discharge was 2,050 cubic metres per second on 16 September 1960.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isère (river)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Allobroges held the land between the Rhône, the <b>Isère</b> and Lake Geneva, in the modern Isère and Savoy.</blockquote><a href="/state/allobroges">Allobroges</a></li><li><blockquote>Strabo names the Cavares as the leading people between the Durance and the <b>Isère</b>, east of the Rhône.</blockquote><a href="/state/cavares">Cavares</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ceutrones held the upper <b>Isère</b> valley, the Tarentaise in Savoy, below the Little St Bernard pass.</blockquote><a href="/state/ceutrones">Ceutrones</a></li><li><blockquote>Strabo places the Medulli on the highest mountains above the confluence of the <b>Isère</b> and the Rhône, and they are generally located in the Maurienne, the valley of the Arc in Savoy.</blockquote><a href="/state/medulli">Medulli</a></li></ul></section>
